    [Refactor] [Observability]Extract ms_metrics subpackage from observability (#19224)
    
    ### What this PR does / why we need it?
    Isolate the MS Service Metric integration (PR #13783) into
    observability/ms_metrics/ so it is clearly separated from the
    runtime_config/runtime_guard control plane contributed by PR #16849.
    
    - One YAML file per feature in ms_metrics/config/ and one handler module
    per feature in ms_metrics/handlers/, making new features additive.
    - Extract shared building blocks into handlers/common.py: a frozen
    `MetricSpec` dataclass (name, type, label names), `register_metrics`
    (registers a feature's specs once per recorder instance and re-registers
    when the recorder is replaced), and `safe_recording` (failure isolation
    so inference results are never affected). `common.py` is the only module
    importing `ms_service_metric`.
    - Both registration and recording are skipped with a debug log when the
    metric recorder is unavailable, so a missing recorder can neither crash
    nor flood warnings.
    - Update setup.py entry point and package_data, docs, and unit tests to
    the new layout; YAML handler references now point to
    vllm_ascend.observability.ms_metrics.handlers:function_name.
    
    - Update setup.py entry point and package_data, docs, and unit tests to
    the new layout; YAML handler references now point to
    vllm_ascend.observability.ms_metrics.handlers:function_name.
    
    ### Does this PR introduce _any_ user-facing change?
    YAML handler references change from
    `vllm_ascend.observability.handlers:...` to
    `vllm_ascend.observability.ms_metrics.handlers:...`, and the
    `ms_service_metric.providers` entry point in setup.py moves accordingly.
    No functional behavior change: same metrics, same values.
    
    ### How was this patch tested?
    - Unit tests (CPU): added/updated
    `tests/ut/observability/test_ms_metrics_provider.py`
    (10 tests), covering provider registration and packaged YAML discovery,
    the
    setup.py entry point and package data, entry-point loading without the
    metric
    core installed, YAML symbol existence via AST checks against the current
    source, eplb handler rank-zero recording, register-once per recorder,
    and the
      recorder-unavailable skip path. Verified with:
    `pytest tests/ut/observability/test_ms_metrics_provider.py` (10 passed).
    
    - End-to-end validation (NPU): deployed a vLLM Ascend service with
    `ms-service-metric on`, sent inference requests, and confirmed the base
    metrics (scheduler / model runner phases) are exposed on `/metrics` and
      rendered correctly in Grafana. See screenshots below.
    
    - The eplb hotness metrics are covered by the unit tests above
    (registration,
    rank-zero gating, None-recorder skip) but have not yet been re-validated
    on
      NPU hardware; I can add an EPLB-enabled run before merge if needed.
    
    - CI: pre-commit (ruff, mypy) and unit-test jobs passed on the PR.
    
